{
  "id": "py-12-and-or-not",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u2",
  "order": 12,
  "title": "and, or, not, and truthy values",
  "summary": "Combine tests with and, or and not, and learn which values count as True.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "expr",
    "branch",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "You can join tests together:\n\n- `a and b` is True only when **both** are True\n- `a or b` is True when **at least one** is True\n- `not a` flips True and False\n\nPython is lazy in a useful way: `and` stops at the first False part and `or` stops at the first True part. The rest is **never worked out** (the working it out panel shows it as skipped). That lets you write safe tests like `count > 0 and total / count > 5`.\n\nAny value can be a test, not just True or False. These count as False: `False`, `0`, `0.0`, the empty string `\"\"` and `None` (Python's value for \"nothing\"). Everything else counts as True, even `\"0\"` and `\" \"`.",
    "analogy": "and is a door with two locks: it only opens if both keys turn. or is a room with two doors: one open door is enough to get in. not is a sign saying \"the opposite\"."
  },
